  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crimes scan src/api/state.ts --format json` returns exactly one `singular_plural_type_mismatch` finding — `crime_00024`, low severity, confidence 0.70, covering two declarations at lines 24–27.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n```\n\"plural name, singular type: `users: User` @L24\"\n\"singular name, array type: `invoice: Invoice[]` @L27\"\n\"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\"\n```\n\nSummary, verbatim: `\"2 declarations whose plural form disagrees with the annotated type's array shape.\"`\nFix shape, verbatim: `\"make the name agree with the type — `users` for array, `user` for scalar\"`\n\nThe source confirms both:\n\n```ts\n24: export const users: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n27: export const invoice: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\nNothing in the repo imports `src/api/state.ts`, so no call site constrains either choice — the tiebreaker has to come from the declaration itself.\n\n---\n\n## `users: User` @L24 — **rename (option a)**\n\nThe intended meaning lives on the **type side**. Two independent signals say \"one user\" and only the identifier says \"many\":\n\n- the annotation is `User`, not `User[]`\n- the initializer is a single object literal, `{ id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" }` — not an array\n\nOption (b) — widening to `users: User[] = [{ id: \"u_1\", … }]` — would require rewriting the initializer to invent a collection that the code never had. That's a behaviour change dressed up as a naming fix. The name is the one thing here with no evidence behind it, so the name is what moves.\n\n**Caveat:** the obvious target name is taken. Line 33 already declares `export const user: User = { id: \"u_2\", email: \"x@y.z\" }` (a negative control the detector correctly does *not* flag). A plain rename to `user` is a redeclaration error. Pick a name that says which user this is — `seedUser`, `currentUser`, `defaultUser` — or, if the two declarations genuinely represent the same thing, delete one.\n\n## `invoice: Invoice[]` @L27 — **rename (option a)**\n\nSame verdict, same reasoning, mirrored. The intended meaning is on the **type side**:\n\n- the annotation is `Invoice[]`\n- the initializer is `[]` — an empty array, which is only coherent for a collection\n\nOption (b) — narrowing to `invoice: Invoice` — is strictly worse: there is no single invoice value to narrow *to*. You'd have to fabricate one, since `[]` has no scalar counterpart. The array shape is load-bearing; the singular name is the accident.\n\n**Same caveat:** line 34 already has `export const invoices: Invoice[] = []` — a byte-for-byte duplicate of line 27 apart from the name. Here the honest fix is probably deletion rather than renaming: `invoice` and `invoices` are the same empty array of the same type. Renaming produces a redeclaration; merging produces one correct declaration.\n\n---\n\n## Two things worth knowing before you touch this file\n\n**Both flagged declarations collide with the detector's own negative controls.** Lines 33–34 exist specifically so the detector can prove it *doesn't* fire on `user: User` and `invoices: Invoice[]`. That makes the mechanical fix (`users` → `user`, `invoice` → `invoices`) a compile error in both cases. An agent applying `fix_shape` literally would break the file. Rename to a distinguishing name, or consolidate.\n\n**Don't reach for option (b) on either one, and be aware the types are already contested.** The same file carries two high-severity `contract_drift` findings — `crime_00032` (`User`, src/api/state.ts:4 vs src/billing.ts:4, disagreeing on `createdAt`) and `crime_00033` (`Invoice`, src/api/state.ts:9 vs src/billing.ts:19, disagreeing on 6 fields). Changing the type side to satisfy a naming detector means editing a declaration that is already out of sync with its twin in `billing.ts`. The naming fix should stay a naming fix.\n\n**If the divergence were intentional**, the finding's own escape hatch applies — from `suggested_actions`, verbatim: `\"If the project intentionally diverges, add the name to `detectors.options.singular_plural_type_mismatch.allowedNames`.\"` It isn't intentional here; this file's header comment says `\"Intentionally bad: boolean-naming and plural-mismatch demonstrations.\"` — these declarations are fixture material, deliberately wrong so the detector has something to catch. Fixing them for real would delete the test case.",
